# Run-day manifest: the lines of the published files
#
# The rules and tools of the 12 November 2026 Sepolia run were fixed before the run and listed, with their SHA-256 and
# size in bytes, in one manifest file (RUN_DAY_MANIFEST.sha256, 23 lines). The SHA-256 of that manifest,
# 8a2ff62b04a01042e8a523a240eda58d3eb183841b46d8eb1c40803a9d22e04f, was e-mailed to the corresponding author's own
# mailbox before the run (received 07:18:25 IST, 12 November 2026; see the Data Availability Statement of the paper).
#
# The manifest also lists two internal planning files (R2_REVISION_PLAN.md and R2_SOP.md) that are not published.
# So a reader can check each published file against its manifest line below, but cannot recompute the manifest hash
# from this repository.
#
# Each entry is the manifest line unchanged (SHA-256, size, path in the authors' folder), then "=>" and the path of the
# same file in this repository. Every published file was copied byte for byte and its SHA-256 checked after copying.
# Check one file: sha256sum <path in this repository>, and compare with the first field.
#
# Line endings. The files under run_day/ and results/sepolia_v1.1_deployment.json are stored without line-ending
# conversion (see .gitattributes), so their bytes are the same on every system. contracts/DIDRegistry.sol and the
# scripts were committed at 73b4cca with LF line endings. The manifest hashed the authors' Windows checkout, in which
# git wrote contracts/DIDRegistry.sol with CRLF line endings: its line matches after converting LF to CRLF. The five
# scripts have LF endings in that checkout too, so their lines match as committed. The release zip and a checkout
# without line-ending conversion hold the contract with LF endings. To check it against its line:
#   python3 -c "import hashlib; b = open('contracts/DIDRegistry.sol', 'rb').read().replace(b'\r\n', b'\n'); print(hashlib.sha256(b.replace(b'\n', b'\r\n')).hexdigest())"
#
